  {
   "id": "OQ8vYh0BCwAJ",
   "from": "Chris Watson",
   "author": "dadcb4fd6a01f8d1",
   "date": "2020-08-26T15:58:35Z",
   "subject": "Re: [brainGraph-help] Best way of computing graph attributes by lobe",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif\">It depends on what you want to compute, exactly.</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif\">* If you are interested in a subnetwork consisting only of regions for a given lobe, then yes your proposed approach would be fine.</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif\">* If you want to be sure that inter-lobe connections are not ignored, then it would be better to construct the entire graphs and then create a data.table w/ vertex-level metrics, and calculate by lobe using data.table syntax. e.g., something like</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif\">DT &lt;- vertex_attr_dt(g)</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif\">DT[, mean(E.nodal.wt), by=lobe]</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif\">Chris<br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Wed, Aug 26, 2020 at 12:47 AM &#39;Gershon&#39; via brainGraph-help &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">brainGr...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
